layout: layouts/base.njk
title: "Page not found"
description: "That address does not exist on this site. The main sections are listed here."
permalink: /404.html
noindex: true
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{% from "components/container.njk" import container %}

{#
  THE 404 PAGE, AND ITS REAL JOB IS THE STATUS CODE.

  Before this file existed there was no 404 page at all, in src or in the
  output, and that is not a cosmetic gap. Cloudflare Pages serves a custom
  404.html from the output root with a 404 status when one exists, and with none
  present it falls back to serving index.html with a 200 for every unmatched
  path. So every nonsense address returned 200 and the home page, at an
  unlimited number of addresses. Measured 2026-08-16 on
  circulation-studio.pages.dev: /sitemap.xml and an invented path both returned
  200 with the home page body.

  WHY THAT COMPOUNDS, AND WHY IT HAD TO BE FIXED BEFORE CUTOVER. src/_redirects
  still carries no migration map. At cutover, every old indexed URL with no rule
  would have returned 200 and the home page, so Search Console would report them
  as live pages and the missing redirects would be invisible. A 404 is the
  signal the map is collected FROM. Serving a 200 in its place does not just
  fail to help, it destroys the evidence.

  PERMALINK IS /404.html AND NOT /public/404.html, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF
  robots.txt, _headers AND _redirects. Those three route through public/ because
  Vite discards unreferenced files from the output root, and publicDir is copied
  verbatim. Verbatim is exactly wrong here: this is an HTML page, and every HTML
  file in Eleventy's output IS a Vite input, so it gets its stylesheet and script
  references rewritten to the hashed /assets/ builds like any other page. Routed
  through public/ it would be copied unprocessed and would ship pointing at
  /src/css/main.css, which does not exist in the output. It would render
  unstyled. Confirmed by building it both ways, see DEPLOYMENT.md.

  The rule is about file type, not about location: non-HTML goes through
  public/, HTML does not.

  noindex: true, on every deploy including production. The 404 RESPONSE carries
  the status code and needs no help, but /404.html is itself a real file at a
  real address that returns 200 if anyone requests it directly, and a page
  reading "page not found" is not one to have in an index. base.njk's `noindex`
  front matter is the per-page way in, separate from the per-deploy rule, which
  is why this survives cutover.

  FULL CHROME, deliberately. Someone arriving here came from a search result or
  a link that used to work, and this is the only chance to keep them. The
  masthead and colophon come from the layout, so every destination on the site
  is already one click away; what this page adds is the four sections named in
  the reader's own terms rather than as a nav bar they have to notice.

  No search box, because there is no search on this site and a box that goes
  nowhere is worse than no box. No "sorry", because the reader wants the page
  and not an apology.
#}

{% call container(class="cs-notfound") %}
  <p class="cs-notfound__code">404</p>
  <h1 class="cs-notfound__headline">That page is not here.</h1>
  <p class="cs-notfound__lede">
    The address may have changed, or it may never have existed. Nothing is
    broken on your end. Here is the rest of the site.
  </p>

  <ul class="cs-notfound__list">
    {% for item in nav.primary %}
      <li>
        <a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a>
      </li>
    {% endfor %}
    {% for item in nav.footer %}
      <li>
        <a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a>
      </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
{% endcall %}
